    fullDescription: "Krauma is a modern, stylish geothermal spa in Borgarfjörður, West Iceland, about 1.5 hours' drive from Reykjavik, and it has a remarkable source: its water comes straight from Deildartunguhver, the most powerful hot spring in Europe, which gushes out 180 litres of near-boiling water every second. That scalding water is cooled with pure glacial meltwater to create six pools at different, perfect bathing temperatures, alongside two steam rooms and a bracing cold tub for the traditional hot-and-cold ritual. The setting is peaceful and scenic, and the whole place has a clean, contemporary design that feels a world away from the tourist crush of the Blue Lagoon, while remaining far more of a genuine local-style spa. Entry is around $35. It works beautifully as part of a West Iceland day trip, easily combined with the neighbouring Deildartunguhver hot spring itself, the lava-field waterfalls of Hraunfossar and the historic village of Reykholt, where the saga writer Snorri Sturluson lived. For travellers heading into West Iceland, or anyone wanting a modern, uncrowded geothermal spa fed by an extraordinary natural source, Krauma is a relaxing and rewarding stop, and a lovely way to warm up after a day exploring the region's waterfalls and history.",
